WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:05.259
Even the minor characters on 2023's "Night
Court" have long acting resumes.

00:00:05.259 --> 00:00:10.809
From "Columbo" to "Star Trek 3," here's where
you may have seen these familiar faces before.

00:00:10.809 --> 00:00:14.152
Melissa Rauch plays Judge Abby Stone on the
new "Night Court."

00:00:14.152 --> 00:00:18.289
She's the daughter of Judge Harry Stone, who
previously occupied the bench for the Manhattan

00:00:18.289 --> 00:00:19.970
Criminal Court's night shift.

00:00:19.970 --> 00:00:24.039
Abby brings the same approach to jurisprudence
as her late father, treating those who appear

00:00:24.039 --> 00:00:26.686
before her as people first and foremost.

00:00:26.686 --> 00:00:30.050
"Also they gave me this fancy wooden hammer
so I get to do what I want."

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:32.680
Rauch was a huge fan of the original "Night
Court."

00:00:32.680 --> 00:00:34.260
She shared during an interview with Glamour,

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:37.980
"I have such a distinct memory of watching
those opening credits with the iconic theme

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:38.980
song playing.

00:00:38.980 --> 00:00:42.120
I vividly remember being transfixed by it
in my childhood bedroom."

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:47.320
Rauch got her start in the 2006 film "Delirious"
alongside Steve Buscemi.

00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.300
She followed up her big-screen debut with
roles in the comedy shows "12 Miles of Bad

00:00:51.300 --> 00:00:54.149
Road" and the American remake of "Kath &amp; Kim."

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:58.440
Rauch got her big break when she joined the
cast of the CBS sitcom "The Big Bang Theory"

00:00:58.440 --> 00:01:01.920
as recurring character Bernadette during the
show's third season.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:05.619
She was promoted to the main cast the following
season and played Bernadette until the show's

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:07.350
conclusion in 2019.

00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:12.299
While she has worked primarily in television,
Rauch has had several high-profile film projects

00:01:12.299 --> 00:01:13.299
as well.

00:01:13.299 --> 00:01:18.020
In 2015, she starred as Hope Ann Gregory in
the sports comedy "The Bronze," and the following

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:21.740
year she provided the voice of Francine in
"Ice Age: Collision Course."

00:01:21.740 --> 00:01:26.241
Her voiceover resume also includes playing
Harley Quinn in the 2017 animated film "Batman

00:01:26.241 --> 00:01:27.940
and Harley Quinn."

00:01:27.940 --> 00:01:31.820
"Night Court" is her first major project since
the end of "The Big Bang Theory."

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:37.100
British actor and singer India de Beaufort
plays Olivia, the assistant district attorney

00:01:37.100 --> 00:01:38.810
assigned to Abby's court.

00:01:38.810 --> 00:01:42.840
In an interview with The Mary Sue, de Beaufort
discussed why she was so excited to be cast

00:01:42.840 --> 00:01:45.939
in the "Night Court" reboot and how it continues
the legacy of the original.

00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:50.229
"I think what we've done with 'Night Court'
is we've carried over a place to disappear.

00:01:50.229 --> 00:01:54.600
The joy, the fun, the lighthearted spirit,
but we've also made sure that as much as you're

00:01:54.600 --> 00:01:58.369
laughing, you're also occasionally crying
and you're invested in these characters while

00:01:58.369 --> 00:02:02.200
you're kind of being taken out of the everyday
life, which for the moment put a lot of us

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:03.490
very serious."

00:02:03.490 --> 00:02:08.020
De Beaufort got her start on British television
in 2003 when she was cast as India on the

00:02:08.020 --> 00:02:10.530
family comedy "The Basil Brush Show."

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.980
After leaving the kid-centric series in 2005
she had a starring role alongside Simon Pegg

00:02:14.980 --> 00:02:20.480
in David Schwimmer's 2007 comedy film "Run,
Fatboy, Run" before making her American television

00:02:20.480 --> 00:02:24.760
debut playing Sire Records employee Miranda
Stone in "One Tree Hill."

00:02:24.760 --> 00:02:28.790
Since then, de Beaufort has appeared in numerous
television comedies, including "Chuck," "How

00:02:28.790 --> 00:02:30.900
I Met Your Mother," and "2 Broke Girls."

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:35.280
She also appeared in the HBO comedy "Veep"
as Brie Ramachandran.

00:02:35.463 --> 00:02:40.720
New Orleans native John Larroquette has been
acting in film and television since the 1970s.

00:02:40.720 --> 00:02:45.069
In the "Night Court" reboot, Larroquette reprises
his role as Dan Fielding from the original,

00:02:45.069 --> 00:02:48.370
a performance which netted him four Primetime
Emmy awards.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:52.489
In the pilot episode of the revival, Dan,
no longer a district attorney, is working

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:56.870
as a process server before being convinced
to return to court as a public defender by

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:58.159
Abby Stone.

00:02:58.159 --> 00:03:02.360
Larroquette was initially apprehensive about
reprising his role but was ultimately intrigued

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.599
by the idea of exploring how his character
had grown.

00:03:05.599 --> 00:03:07.390
He said during a TV Line interview,

00:03:07.390 --> 00:03:11.099
"How often does an actor get a chance to revisit
some character that he played three and a

00:03:11.099 --> 00:03:12.650
half decades ago?

00:03:12.650 --> 00:03:17.995
Larroquette got his start as the narrator
of "The Texas Chain Saw Massacre" in 1974.

00:03:17.995 --> 00:03:22.480
"For them, an idyllic summer afternoon drive
became a nightmare."

00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:27.090
His first major film role came in 1981 when
he played Captain Stillman in the Bill Murray

00:03:27.090 --> 00:03:28.780
war comedy "Stripes."

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:33.159
In the 1980s, in addition to his role on "Night
Court," he played the Klingon officer Maltz

00:03:33.159 --> 00:03:37.890
in "Star Trek III: The Search for Spock,"
and he was also in "Twilight Zone: The Movie,"

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:40.000
appearing in the segment "Time Out."

00:03:40.000 --> 00:03:44.010
After "Night Court," Larroquette got his own
sitcom about a night shift on NBC: "The John

00:03:44.010 --> 00:03:48.310
Larroquette Show," in which he played recovering
alcoholic John Hemingway.

00:03:48.310 --> 00:03:51.750
Following the show's cancellation after four
seasons, he continued to appear regularly

00:03:51.750 --> 00:03:56.540
on television, popping up in shows like "The
West Wing," "The Practice," "Arrested Development,"

00:03:56.540 --> 00:03:57.540
and "House."

00:03:57.652 --> 00:04:03.670
One-named actor Lacretta plays Donna "Gurgs"
Gurganous, the new bailiff in Abby's courtroom.

00:04:03.670 --> 00:04:07.140
When she read the script for the new show,
she was drawn to how uniquely weird Gurgs

00:04:07.140 --> 00:04:08.440
seemed to be.

00:04:08.440 --> 00:04:12.090
Speaking to Theater Mania about the experience
of playing Gurgs, Lacretta said

00:04:12.090 --> 00:04:15.640
"She's smart — she's just different from
our typical big, Black woman, and I really

00:04:15.640 --> 00:04:16.910
want to embrace that.

00:04:16.910 --> 00:04:20.630
I've worked and gotten to the point in my
skill and talent where I'm able to be honest

00:04:20.630 --> 00:04:23.545
as well and not just be weird for the sake
of being weird."

00:04:23.545 --> 00:04:24.685
"Welcome to Night Court."

00:04:25.060 --> 00:04:29.410
Over the course of her relatively short career,
Lacretta has worked primarily in theater.

00:04:29.410 --> 00:04:33.320
She played Gary Coleman in the puppet musical
"Avenue Q" and was an ensemble member for

00:04:33.320 --> 00:04:38.133
the first national tour of the Trey Parker
and Matt Stone musical comedy "The Book of Mormon."

00:04:38.133 --> 00:04:42.630
She also originated the role of Levora in
the jukebox musical "Disaster!," which premiered

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:45.000
on Broadway in 2016.

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.789
Kapil Talwalkar plays Abby's clerk Neil in
the new "Night Court."

00:04:48.789 --> 00:04:52.780
In an interview with Bleeding Cool, he said
that being cast on a multi-camera sitcom was

00:04:52.780 --> 00:04:56.889
something he had always dreamed about as a
kid before going on to praise star and executive

00:04:56.889 --> 00:04:58.380
producer Melissa Rauch.

00:04:58.380 --> 00:05:00.870
"Melissa is a unicorn of a human being.

00:05:00.870 --> 00:05:03.460
She creates the most amazing work environment
ever.

00:05:03.460 --> 00:05:07.330
She's so positive, and she's like her character
in so many ways that she plays."

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:12.210
Talwaker's breakout role came in 2020 when
he was cast as Tobin in the NBC musical comedy

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:13.990
"Zoey's Extraordinary Playlist."

00:05:13.990 --> 00:05:18.790
If you're a fan of The CW, then you'll probably
recognize Talwalkar from the "Charmed" reboot

00:05:18.790 --> 00:05:22.020
— he played Dev across six episodes in 2022.

00:05:22.125 --> 00:05:26.919
Los Angeles native Kimia Behpoornia plays
Maggie in the "Night Court" reboot.

00:05:26.919 --> 00:05:30.960
She has been active in the industry since
2012, when she played Shelley in the short

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:31.974
film "Treasure."

00:05:31.974 --> 00:05:35.399
Since her acting debut, she has appeared regularly
on television.

00:05:35.399 --> 00:05:41.070
In 2017, she plied her trade in two episodes
of the loosely scripted comedy series "Drive Share."

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:45.449
She also appeared in her first feature film
that year, acting alongside Brie Larson, Samuel

00:05:45.449 --> 00:05:49.870
L. Jackson, and Joan Cusack in the Netflix
original movie "Unicorn Store."

00:05:49.870 --> 00:05:54.990
Her career really began to take off in 2019
when she played Rosie in the short-lived NBC

00:05:54.990 --> 00:05:58.130
sitcom "Abby's," appearing in all ten episodes.

00:05:58.130 --> 00:06:01.620
Two years later, she returned to the big screen,
playing the part of Marie in the adventure

00:06:01.620 --> 00:06:07.211
comedy "Hero Mode" and landing the lead role
of Emilou Paunch in the mockumentary film "Marathon."

00:06:07.211 --> 00:06:11.840
She had a major supporting role in the third
season of "Atypical," popped up in the HBO

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:16.430
comedy "Hacks," and recurred on the Hulu original
comedy series "Reboot" before appearing in

00:06:16.430 --> 00:06:18.919
the first season of "Night Court" in 2023.

00:06:18.919 --> 00:06:23.665
She is also currently providing the voice
of Watt in the PBS Kids show "City Island."

00:06:24.014 --> 00:06:29.340
Actor and musician Dimiter Marinov appears
in "Night Court" as Nikolai, the court's mysterious

00:06:29.340 --> 00:06:30.470
maintenance man.

00:06:30.470 --> 00:06:34.580
Per his website, he was originally a classical
violinist in Bulgaria.

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:38.919
Marinov began acting in ad campaigns when
he moved to the United States in 1990.

00:06:38.919 --> 00:06:43.800
His first major role came in the 2012 war
thriller "Act of Valor," where he shared the

00:06:43.800 --> 00:06:45.960
screen with real-life Navy SEALs.

00:06:45.960 --> 00:06:50.400
He followed up this role with small parts
in numerous television shows including "NCIS,"

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:53.759
"Brooklyn Nine-Nine," and the Marvel series
"Agent Carter."

00:06:53.759 --> 00:06:58.360
Marinov continued to work steadily in television
into the late 2010s, landing roles in the

00:06:58.360 --> 00:07:03.060
Zach Galifianakis comedy series "Baskets"
and HBO's dark comedy "Barry."

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:07.759
His big break would arrive in 2018 when he
was cast as Dr. Shirley's cellist in the historical

00:07:07.759 --> 00:07:09.300
drama film "Green Book."

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:13.330
The film would go on to win best picture at
the Academy Awards, and Marinov became the

00:07:13.330 --> 00:07:16.760
first Bulgarian actor to walk the red carpet
at the Oscars.

00:07:16.760 --> 00:07:21.259
More recently, he played the hotel manager
in the 2021 crime drama "The Little Things,"

00:07:21.259 --> 00:07:24.273
sharing the screen with Hollywood heavyweight
Denzel Washington.

00:07:24.563 --> 00:07:30.660
Iris Bahr appears in the retooled "Night Court"
as the supposed psychic Esmeralda the Magnificent.

00:07:30.660 --> 00:07:34.420
She's been active in film and TV since the
turn of the millennium, first appearing as

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:38.270
a Starfleet cadet in a 2001 episode of "Star
Trek: Voyager."

00:07:38.270 --> 00:07:42.191
The New Yorker spent most of her early career
appearing in one-off episodes of TV shows,

00:07:42.191 --> 00:07:46.360
including the Kevin James sitcom "The King
of Queens" and the final season of the classic

00:07:46.360 --> 00:07:48.849
detective show "Columbo" in 2003.

00:07:48.849 --> 00:07:54.240
Her film debut came in 2006 when she landed
the role of Amy Butlin in "Larry the Cable

00:07:54.240 --> 00:07:55.330
Guy: Health Inspector."

00:07:55.330 --> 00:07:59.630
She snagged her first major movie role four
years later when she played Iris Reisen in

00:07:59.630 --> 00:08:01.810
the horror film "The Last Exorcism."

00:08:01.810 --> 00:08:06.080
The same year, she created and starred in
the comedy series "Svetlana", which ran for

00:08:06.080 --> 00:08:07.740
two seasons before being canceled.

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:12.840
One of her most recognizable characters is
Rachel from Larry David's "Curb Your Enthusiasm."

00:08:12.840 --> 00:08:16.620
She played the devout Jew in three episodes
of the long-running HBO comedy.

00:08:16.620 --> 00:08:21.659
More recently, Bahr appeared in two episodes
of ABC's "The Conners" and played Perla in

00:08:21.659 --> 00:08:23.776
the HBO comedy series "Hacks."

00:08:24.106 --> 00:08:28.190
Brendan Jennings plays Howie in the second
episode of the new "Night Court."

00:08:28.190 --> 00:08:32.660
A product of the illustrious Second City in
Chicago, Jennings' improv background has gotten

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:38.409
him numerous roles in TV comedies since 2013,
when he appeared as Walt Koypond in a Season

00:08:38.409 --> 00:08:40.330
5 episode of "Parks and Recreation."

00:08:40.330 --> 00:08:44.390
His other early TV roles include small parts
in "Cougar Town" and "New Girl."

00:08:44.390 --> 00:08:49.029
In 2018, he briefly appeared in the "Breaking
Bad" prequel series "Better Call Saul" as

00:08:49.029 --> 00:08:53.510
an office worker, then, two years later, he
was cast in an episode of "Curb Your Enthusiasm."

00:08:53.510 --> 00:08:58.970
His most recent roles prior to "Night Court"
were as Dale in "A.P. Bio," Dennis in "Superstore,"

00:08:58.970 --> 00:09:02.760
and as Massacre Mike in the Netflix Comedy
series "The Woman in the House Across the

00:09:02.760 --> 00:09:04.605
Street from the Girl in the Window."

00:09:04.875 --> 00:09:09.470
Will Hines plays a customer that's served,
in multiple ways, by Dan Fielding at the beginning

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:11.470
of the pilot episode of "Night Court."

00:09:11.470 --> 00:09:15.310
Like Brendan Jennings, Hines got his start
in the world of improv comedy.

00:09:15.310 --> 00:09:19.290
He's an alumnus of the Upright Citizens Brigade
in New York City and has written two books

00:09:19.290 --> 00:09:21.459
on improv, according to his website.

00:09:21.459 --> 00:09:25.430
After starring in some comedic web series,
he appeared with Donald Glover in the Derrick

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:30.279
Comedy feature film "Mystery Team," playing
Mr. Stevens alongside several other established

00:09:30.279 --> 00:09:31.620
improv actors.

00:09:31.620 --> 00:09:35.820
Later in his career, Hines landed roles in
a number of TV comedies, including "Broad

00:09:35.820 --> 00:09:40.120
City," "Community," and "Crazy Ex-Girlfriend,"
and he was featured in sketches on "Inside

00:09:40.120 --> 00:09:40.804
Amy Schumer."

00:09:40.804 --> 00:09:44.600
He is perhaps best known for playing Carl
Kurm on "Brooklyn Nine-Nine" and Professor

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.890
Bennett on "The Sex Lives of College Girls."

00:09:46.890 --> 00:09:51.320
Podcast fans may also know him from a series
of appearances on "Comedy Bang Bang."

00:09:51.320 --> 00:09:56.310
In addition to acting, Hines has released
two albums as one half of the band Wayne &amp; Rodney,

00:09:56.310 --> 00:09:59.180
which he created with fellow improviser Joel
Spence.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:03.197
He also runs the online improv school The
World's Greatest Improv School.

00:10:03.197 --> 00:10:07.280
Al Bayan hadn't been acting for very long
when he appeared in the second episode of

00:10:07.280 --> 00:10:08.813
"Night Court" in 2023.

00:10:08.813 --> 00:10:13.900
He got his start in the industry in 2018 when
he played a coroner in an episode of "9-1-1"

00:10:13.900 --> 00:10:17.829
and since then he has appeared regularly on
the small screen, popping up in a number of

00:10:17.829 --> 00:10:18.829
big shows.

00:10:18.829 --> 00:10:23.649
He followed up his debut role by appearing
in 2019 episodes of "Good Girls," "Why Women

00:10:23.649 --> 00:10:28.360
Kill," and the Oprah Winfrey Network legal
drama "All Rise."

00:10:28.360 --> 00:10:31.839
In 2020, Bayan landed his biggest role to
date, appearing in HBO's critically acclaimed

00:10:31.839 --> 00:10:34.630
dystopian science fiction series "Westworld."

00:10:34.630 --> 00:10:38.284
He played a behavior tech in the Season 3
episode "The Winter Line."

